This folder contains unique cluster configurations used in the SE
performance tests.

It contains sub-folders for performance experiments, where the configuration may be changed
for any run, and performance regressions, where the configuration is expected to be immutable
for its lifetime.

Each cluster configuration is known by it's unique file name. It is very important that
this filename not change once it has been used, as this filename gets saved with the 
performance results and will be used in queries to filter and return particular 
performance data.

The naming convention used is trying to clearly identify the main parameters of the
cluster configuration, being number of nodes, partitions, threads and shared memory.
This is more useful and reusable than a naming convention of the purpose of the config
file. e.g. It is more useful to have file named cluster-2N32P64T485G.config than one
named tpch-sf1000.config as the former could be reused for other purposes whereas a
user needs to open the latter and look at its contents to get more information.

The encoding used in the file names is as follows. We use an example cluster config
filename of: cluster-2N32P64T485G.config
In this example:
    2N      means 2 nodes in the cluster
    32P     means 32 partitions per node
    64T     means 64 threads per node
    485G    means 485GB of shared memory per node

Any additional clarifying remarks can be appended after this encoding and separated
from it by a dash. e.g. cluster-2N32P64T485G-withParamXYZ.config

Please think carefully about how cluster configs are named as they are saved in the
database with their configuration to guard against the case where a regular repeating
cluster config is unknowingly changed in such a way that the results of other jobs using
it are affected. We need to ensure that configurations are consistent and hence they should
be treated as being immutable.